**Read:** [Nano-to-Macro Transport Processes — MIT 2.57](../../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/sources/Nano-to-Macro-Transport-MIT-2-57.lingua#r_1YF7TD689ZRGQ51D4B3NGVHZQG), the full carrier-transport progression; [Functional material response — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Functional-Response-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_6WMQKCE9PFYYETKPTR50DH6RTM), Thermal and Electrical Conduction; [Solid State Physics — David Tong](../sources/Solid-State-Physics-Tong.lingua#r_2G2CQ9C5QDEBTQ2YNMYQ5QK3MB), phonons and electron dynamics.

Thermal conductivity describes energy flow under specified conditions; heat capacity describes stored energy per temperature change. A high capacity does not ensure rapid heat spreading. For an isotropic kinetic approximation, κ≈C vℓ/3 when C is volumetric capacity and v and mean free path ℓ represent the relevant carriers. Real spectra require mode-dependent velocities, populations and scattering.

Grains, boundaries, defects, pores and interfaces can change heat transport while also changing strength and electrical response. Distinguish an electronic contribution from vibrational and radiative contributions. The Wiedemann–Franz relation is a conditional electronic transport result, not a rule linking total thermal conductivity to DC conductivity in every solid.

**Work:** Trace units and estimate a mean free path from a declared model. Then read the shared kinetic and interface Records, comparing a bulk coupon with a film and explaining where the same fitted conductivity ceases to represent the same measurement.

